/tmp/tmp.BYvlIxb7Wm/basicAlgo.tex:86:      If $(K, K') \in \Psi_g(\tau', \tau)$ there exists the distinct sets $C_1, \dots, C_{\tau'}\in K$ and for all $0 \leq i \leq \tau'$ there is a $C \in K'$ s.t. $C$ and $C_i$ do $\tau \& g$-conflict. Combined with above's claim we have that for each set $[C_1]_b, \dots, [C_{\tau'}]_b$ there exists $C \in K'$ s.t. $|C \cap [C_i]_b| \geq \tau$. By definition of $[K]_b$ we have $[C_1]_b, \dots, [C_{\tau'}]_b \in [K]_b$ and hence $([K]_b, K') \in \Psi(\tau', \tau)$. The second implication follows by the same argument.
