Step 0 is now decisive: Appendix A, Lemma “Sequential List Defective Coloring Algorithms” gives a potential-decreasing recoloring method under \(\sum_{x\in L_v}(d_v(x)+1)>\deg(v)\), with at most \(3|E|\) recolorings. The main theorem also gives a deterministic distributed algorithm in its stronger parameter regime, while the paper explicitly warns that its zero-round internal search can be enormous. So I am proceeding only as Track B: the reference recoloring algorithm will be disclosed and measured; the benchmark must earn its difficulty from a compact structural invariant hidden inside a much larger exact coloring computation.
codex
I’ve selected a native Track B construction rather than the initially suggested unconstrained planted coloring. The witness will be a three-term affine polynomial over a prime field whose evaluations form a zero-defect list coloring of an explicit graph. The construction samples the polynomial first, then builds the graph and lists around it. The compact route is an extremal/pigeonhole invariant on the graph’s nonedge pairs; the disclosed exact interpolation/list-recovery algorithm remains polynomial and will be measured separately. This keeps the answer fixed-size while the graph and candidate field grow.
Step 0 supports only Track B. I am using Definition 1's native list-defective coloring object with defect zero and a succinct affine-polynomial coloring certificate; no graph reduction is introduced.
